Host tests
==========

Run on macOS or another modern host with a C compiler:

    sh tests/run.sh

The harness includes the actual screen.c and substitutes only terminal
and allocation interfaces.  Its fake terminal interprets cursor moves,
clearing and standout into a visible character/attribute grid.  Two hundred
deterministic randomized frames verify final screen contents independently
of the production cache algorithm.  Focused assertions verify that an
unchanged frame emits no bytes or flush call, an explicit clear still emits
its terminal operation, and a one-character update stays small.

Other cases cover unknown/unsupported terminals, sanitization, shortening
and disappearing rows, reverse-video changes, prompt-only updates, huge
and tiny window sizes, allocation/mode-setting failures, and restoration
of the caller's original terminal flags across repeated resizes.

These tests require no terminal, kernel-memory permissions, or additional
libraries.  They do not emulate the PDP-11 ABI or replace native builds,
live kernel-statistics checks, real terminal tests, or performance timings.

The script also extracts the actual fixed() formatter from top.c into a
temporary header and tests 29 hand-specified decimal results.  These cover
zero, half-up rounding on both sides of a boundary, carries into the integer
part, 60/100 Hz CPU counters, 16 clicks per KiB, 1024 bytes per KiB, and the
largest unsigned 32-bit counters.  Guards verify the 24-byte field budget.
The fixture contains expected strings, not another formatter implementation.

Host unsigned long can be wider than the PDP-11's 32 bits.  Test inputs are
therefore explicitly limited to 0xffffffff and scales used by native callers;
the host tests alone cannot prove absence of every native integer overflow.

Native formatter regression
---------------------------
On 2.11BSD, run from bsd/top:

    /bin/sh tests/native-fixed.sh

The working directory must be bsd/top so the script can find its sources.
This runs the same 29 golden cases with the actual native compiler, integer
widths and libc.  It extracts fixed() from top.c, compiles with cc -O -i,
and runs the test.  The script uses original Bourne shell syntax and creates
a private /tmp/top-fixed.PID directory; cleanup removes only its generated
files.  It leaves the source tree and installed programs unchanged and
returns a nonzero status if extraction, compilation or testing fails.

Both host and native runs matter.  Native boundary cases caught a libc
unsigned-division corner case that a host with wider integers did not show.
